# ip — real-world examples

## Display all interfaces with addresses

```sh
ip addr
ip -br addr      # brief, one line per interface — great for a quick scan
```

## Display this machine's IP address(es)

```sh
hostname -I
```

## Take the wireless adapter down or up

```sh
ip link set dev wlan0 down
ip link set dev wlan0 up
```

## Set a static IP and netmask

```sh
ip addr add 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0
```

## Remove an IP from an interface

```sh
ip addr del 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0
```

## Remove all IPs from an interface

```sh
ip address flush dev eth0
```

## Display routes

```sh
ip route         # IPv4
ip -6 route      # IPv6
```

## Add a default route via gateway

```sh
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1
```

## Add a route via an interface

```sh
ip route add 192.168.0.0/24 dev eth0
```

## Change your MAC address

```sh
ip link set dev eth0 address aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff
```

## View neighbors (ARP / NDP)

```sh
ip neighbor show
```

## Homelab: which interface will traffic to mediaserver leave from?

```sh
ip route get 10.0.0.42
```

## Homelab: watch link state live while reseating a cable on homelab

```sh
ip monitor link
```

| Subcommand  | What it covers               |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| ip addr     | IP addresses on interfaces   |
| ip link     | interface state, MAC, MTU    |
| ip route    | routing table                |
| ip neighbor | ARP/NDP cache                |
| ip -br ...  | brief tabular output         |
| ip monitor  | live view of network events  |
